
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Decorator	                        Purpose
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
@description()	                    Documents a declaration
@secure()	                          Protects supported sensitive parameter values
@allowed()	                        Restricts parameter values to a list
@minValue() / @maxValue()	          Constrains integer values
@minLength() / @maxLength()	        Constrains string or array length
@metadata()	                        Adds custom metadata
@discriminator()	                  Identifies variants of a tagged union
@sealed()	                          Treats likely object-property typos as errors
@export()	                          Exposes a variable, type, or function for import
@batchSize()	                      Controls sequential deployment batches
@retryOn()	                        Retries resource deployment for specified errors
@onlyIfNotExists()	                Skips creation when a resource already exists
@nullIfNotFound()	                  Returns null for a missing existing resource


---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Declaration	             Examples of supported decorators
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
param	                   @description(), @secure(), @minValue(), @maxValue(), @minLength(), 
                         @maxLength(), @allowed(), @metadata(), @discriminator(), @sealed()

output	                 @description(), @minValue(), @maxValue(), @minLength(), @maxLength(),
                         @metadata(), @discriminator(), @sealed(), @secure()

resource	               @description(), @batchSize(), @retryOn(), @onlyIfNotExists(), @nullIfNotFound()

module	                 @description(), @batchSize() 

var	                     @description(), @metadata(), @export()

type	                   @description(), @minValue(), @maxValue(), @minLength(), @maxLength(), 
                         @metadata(), @discriminator(), @sealed(), @secure(), @export()

func	                   @description(), @metadata(), @export()

---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Examples:
---------------------------------------------------------------------------
Adds a description to the parameter.
@description('The Azure region where resources will be deployed.')
param location string = 'eastus'

Documents what the resource does.
@description('Creates a storage account in Azure.')
resource storageAccount 'Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts@2025-06-01' = {
  name: 'examplestorage123'
  location: 'eastus'
  sku: {
    name: 'Standard_LRS'
  }
  kind: 'StorageV2'
}

Marks a string or object parameter as secure so its value isn't 
saved in deployment history or logged as a normal parameter value.
@secure()
param adminPassword string

Requires instanceCount to be between 1 and 10, inclusive.
@minValue(1)
@maxValue(10)
param instanceCount int = 2

Requires the string to contain between 3 and 20 characters.
@minLength(3)
@maxLength(20)
param storagePrefix string

Restricts the parameter to the listed values.
@allowed([
  'eastus'
  'westus2'
  'centralus'
])
param location string = 'eastus'

Adds custom metadata to the parameter.
@metadata({
  purpose: 'Deployment configuration'
  owner: 'Platform team'
})
param environment string = 'dev'

The discriminator tells Bicep which object variant is intended based on kind. 
This is useful for custom-tagged union types. Use this with an object parameter 
that has different variants, identified by a property such as kind.
@discriminator('kind')
param appConfig {
  kind: 'web'
  port: int
} | {
  kind: 'worker'
  queueName: string
}

Elevates certain warnings about likely misspelled property names to errors. For example, 
typing locaton instead of location can be treated as an error.
@sealed()
param appConfig {
  name: string
  location: string
}

Retries deployment up to 10 times when the specified error code occurs. 
Use this for transient errors where retrying can help.
@retryOn(['ResourceNotFound'], 10)
resource storageAccount 'Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts@2025-06-01' = {
  name: 'examplestorage123'
  location: 'eastus'
  sku: {
    name: 'Standard_LRS'
  }
  kind: 'StorageV2'
}

Deploys one storage account at a time instead of deploying all instances in parallel. 
It applies to resources or modules declared with a for loop.
param storageNames array = [
  'examplestorage101'
  'examplestorage102'
  'examplestorage103'
]

@batchSize(1)
resource storageAccounts 'Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts@2025-06-01' = [for storageName in storageNames: {
  name: storageName
  location: 'eastus'
  sku: {
    name: 'Standard_LRS'
  }
  kind: 'StorageV2'
}]

Creates the resource only if it doesn't already exist. If it exists, Azure skips creation and 
leaves it unchanged. This decorator requires Bicep CLI version 0.38.3 or later.
@onlyIfNotExists()
resource storageAccount 'Microsoft.Storage/storageAccounts@2025-06-01' = {
  name: 'examplestorage123'
  location: 'eastus'
  sku: {
    name: 'Standard_LRS'
  }
  kind: 'StorageV2'
}

For an existing resource, returns null if the resource cannot be 
found instead of failing the deployment because it is missing.
@nullIfNotFound()
resource workspace 'Microsoft.OperationalInsights/workspaces@2023-09-01' existing = {
  name: 'my-log-workspace'
}
output workspaceExists bool = workspace != null

















